) according to the input luminance (x-axis).;}0000fA 2D Lookup Texture (LUT) to use for color grading.;}00004MHow much of the lookup texture will contribute to the color grading effect.;}00004èUse "Gaussian" for a faster but non physical depth of field; "Bokeh" for a more realistic but slower depth of field.;}0000\The distance at which the blurring will start.;}000045The distance at which the blurring will reach its maximum radius.;}00004´The maximum radius of the gaussian blur. Values above 1 may show under-sampling artifacts.;}00004²Use higher quality sampling to reduce flickering and improve the overall blur smoothness.;}0000FThe distance to the point of focus.;}00004æThe ratio of aperture (known as f-stop or f-number). The smaller the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.;}00004ÒThe distance between the lens and the film. The larger the value is, the shallower the depth of field is.;}00002The number of aperture blades.;}0000,The curvature of aperture blades. The smaller the value is, the more visible aperture blades are. A value of 1 will make the bokeh perfectly circular.;}0000VThe rotation of aperture blades in degrees.;}00004ÄThe type of grain to use. You can select a preset or provide your own texture by selecting Custom.;}0000xUse the slider to set the strength of the Film Grain effect.;}00004ÚControls the noisiness response curve based on scene luminance. Higher values mean less noise in light areas.;}00004¸A tileable texture to use for the grain. The neutral value is 0.5 where no grain is applied.;}00000Total distortion amount.;}00004TIntensity multiplier on X axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.;}00004TIntensity multiplier on Y axis. Set it to 0 to disable distortion on this axis.;}0000pDistortion center point. 0.5,0.5 is center of the screen;}00004ìControls global screen scaling for the distortion effect. Use this to hide screen borders when using high "Intensity.";}00004ÊUse this to control and apply a hue to the dark tones. This has a more exaggerated effect on shadows.;}00004¢Use this to control and apply a hue to the mid-range tones with a power function.;}00004 Use this to increase and apply a hue to the signal and make highlights brighter.;}00004êThe motion blur technique to use. If you don't need object motion blur, CameraOnly will result in better performance.;}00004ØThe quality of the effect. Lower presets will result in better performance at the expense of visual quality.;}00004OThe strength of the motion blur filter. Acts as a multiplier for velocities.;}0000RSets the maximum length, as a fraction of the screen's full resolution, that the velocity resulting from Camera rotation can have. Lower values will improve performance.;}00006Panini projection distance.;}00002Panini projection crop to fit.;}0000fUse this to control and apply a hue to the shadows.;}0000hUse this to control and apply a hue to the midtones.;}0000lUse this to control and apply a hue to the highlights.;}0000vStart point of the transition between shadows and midtones.;}0000rEnd point of the transition between shadows and midtones.;}0000|Start point of the transition between midtones and highlights.;}0000xEnd point of the transition between midtones and highlights.;}0000:The color to use for shadows.;}0000@The color to use for highlights.;}0000rBalance between the colors in the highlights and shadows.;}00004BSelect a tonemapping algorithm to use for the color grading process.;}00004ÌSpecifies the range reduction mode used when HDR output is enabled and Neutral tonemapping is enabled.;}0000JUse the ACES preset for HDR displays.;}0000Specify how much hue to preserve. Value